WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 4.4: Read-Only DCs and Hybrid Identity. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 4.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:04.250
4, Read-Only DCs and Hybrid Identity.

3
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.700
So far, sa1.

4
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.350
lab has one domain controller, DC01.

5
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.800
In this topic you will see why a domain
needs at least two.

6
00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:19.250
You will meet the read-only domain
controller, or RODC, and learn what it

7
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.300
stores and why branch offices use one.

8
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:27.750
You will promote SRV-CORE to an RODC with
PowerShell, and manage its Password

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.400
Replication Policy in ADUC and PowerShell.

10
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:36.650
Then we look at the cloud: Microsoft Entra
ID, the two sync tools, the sign-in

11
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.700
options, and why the UPN suffix matters.

12
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:43.550
With one domain controller, everything
depends on one server.

13
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:49.800
If DC01 fails, nobody can sign in, and the
AD-integrated DNS zones are gone too.

14
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.850
That is a single point of failure.

15
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.300
Real domains run at least two writable
DCs.

16
00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:02.550
Each holds a full copy of AD DS, accepts
changes, and replicates them to the

17
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.000
others.

18
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.050
If one fails, the other carries on.

19
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.900
On a second joined server, you would
install the AD DS role, then run

20
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.150
Install-ADDSDomainController with the
domain name.

21
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:20.200
Our pod has one writable DC, so this is
shown, not done.

22
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.450
Now picture a branch office.

23
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:29.500
A few users, a slow link to the main
campus, a server in a closet that anyone

24
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.750
could walk off with, and nobody from IT on
site.

25
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.400
A writable DC there is risky.

26
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.050
A read-only domain controller fits better.

27
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.900
It holds every object and attribute,
except account passwords.

28
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:48.750
By default it stores only its own computer
account and its own krbtgt account.

29
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.600
Replication is one-way: nothing replicates
out of an RODC, so a change made there

30
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.050
cannot spread.

31
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.500
Write requests are referred to a writable
DC.

32
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:03.350
So how does anyone sign in at the branch?

33
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.800
Follow dpatel.

34
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:10.250
He signs in at SRV-CORE, but SRV-CORE has
no copy of his password.

35
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.700
So it passes the request over the link to
DC01, a writable DC.

36
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:21.550
DC01 signs him in and checks the Password
Replication Policy, the PRP, for SRV-CORE.

37
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.400
If dpatel is allowed, SRV-CORE stores his
password, and next time it checks him

38
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.450
itself, even if the link is down.

39
00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.900
If he is not allowed, nothing is stored,
and every sign-in needs DC01.

40
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.950
By default, no user passwords are cached.

41
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.200
The PRP has two layers.

42
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.650
Two domain-wide groups sit in the Users
container.

43
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.100
The Allowed RODC Password Replication
Group starts empty.

44
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:53.150
The Denied RODC Password Replication Group
holds powerful groups like Domain Admins,

45
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.400
Enterprise Admins and Schema Admins.

46
00:02:55.650 --> 00:03:00.050
Each RODC also has its own Allowed list
and Denied list.

47
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.900
By default its Allowed list holds the
allowed group, and its Denied list holds

48
00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.750
the denied group plus Administrators and
three operator groups.

49
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.200
Three rules.

50
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:12.650
Deny wins.

51
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.500
The group affects every RODC, the list
just one.

52
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:20.350
And allowed means may be cached, not
cached yet.

53
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.200
Two more RODC features.

54
00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:27.650
Administrator role separation lets you
make a branch user or group the local

55
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.500
administrator of one RODC.

56
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:36.150
They can update a driver or restart the
server, but they get no rights in the

57
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.400
domain or on other DCs.

58
00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.050
You set it with the
DelegatedAdministratorAccountName

59
00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.700
parameter or the Managed By tab.

60
00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.150
Second, read-only DNS.

61
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:50.600
With dash InstallDns, the RODC holds a
read-only copy of zones like sa1.

62
00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.650
lab, so branch clients resolve names
locally.

63
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.300
It does not accept client updates; it
refers them to DC01.

64
00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:04.150
And in real life, never sign in to an RODC
as a Domain Admin.

65
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.200
SRV-CORE is Server Core, so this is
PowerShell from SConfig option fifteen.

66
00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.450
First, Install-WindowsFeature
AD-Domain-Services with dash

67
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:12.900
IncludeManagementTools.

68
00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.350
Then Install-ADDSDomainController.

69
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:15.800
Dash DomainName sa1.

70
00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.850
lab, and the key switch, dash
ReadOnlyReplica.

71
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:23.100
With it, dash SiteName is required, so we
give Default-First-Site-Name.

72
00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:27.750
Dash InstallDns adds the read-only DNS
server, and dash Credential with

73
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Get-Credential SA1 Administrator opens a
sign-in box for that password.

74
00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.450
Then you type the DSRM password twice,
masked.

75
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.500
In the pod, both are the password shown in
your NetLab pod.

76
00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.950
Answer Y, and SRV-CORE restarts as an
RODC.

77
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.800
Back on DC01, open Active Directory Users
and Computers.

78
00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:51.250
Select Domain Controllers, right-click
SRV-CORE, and choose Properties.

79
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.700
The Password Replication Policy tab lists
every group with its setting, Allow or

80
00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:58.150
Deny.

81
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.800
You see the defaults: the allowed group,
and five denied entries.

82
00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.250
Select Add.

83
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.100
A small dialog asks whether to allow or
deny.

84
00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:13.550
Choose Allow passwords for the account to
replicate to this RODC, then type

85
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.800
GG-Students, Check Names, and OK.

86
00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:22.050
GG-Students now shows Allow, but only on
SRV-CORE, not on every RODC in the domain.

87
00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.700
On the same tab, select Advanced.

88
00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:27.750
The Policy Usage tab has a drop-down.

89
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.400
Accounts whose passwords are stored on
this Read-only Domain Controller shows

90
00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:34.250
what is really cached.

91
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.300
At first, only the RODC's own krbtgt
account and its computer account.

92
00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:44.350
The other choice shows accounts that have
been authenticated through the RODC.

93
00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:49.800
Prepopulate Passwords lets you cache an
allowed user before they ever sign in,

94
00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.850
which is handy before a branch opens.

95
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:58.700
And the Resultant Policy tab answers one
question for one account: allow or deny?

96
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.550
Everything you saw in ADUC has a
PowerShell form.

97
00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.800
Get-ADDomainController with dash Filter
star shows both DCs; SRV-CORE has

98
00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.650
IsReadOnly True, and it is a global
catalog too.

99
00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.100

Add-ADDomainControllerPasswordReplicationPolicy

100
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:16.750
with dash AllowedList GG-Students does the
same as Add in ADUC.

101
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.200

Get-ADDomainControllerPasswordReplicationPolicy

102
00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:22.450
with dash Allowed or dash Denied reads the
two lists.

103
00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:23.900
And
Get-ADDomainControllerPasswordReplicationPolicyUsage

104
00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:28.150
with dash RevealedAccounts lists the
passwords that are actually stored.

105
00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:34.400
The names are long, so type the first part
and press Tab to complete them.

106
00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:38.650
Run them on DC01, where the AD module is
installed.

107
00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:40.500
Now the cloud side.

108
00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:46.350
AD DS runs on your own domain controllers
and uses Kerberos, NTLM and LDAP.

109
00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.800
Microsoft Entra ID is a Microsoft cloud
service.

110
00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:54.050
It speaks web protocols such as SAML and
OAuth 2.

111
00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.500
0.

112
00:06:55.750 --> 00:07:00.150
It has no OUs or Group Policy;
administration uses built-in roles.

113
00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.000
Devices can be Entra joined and managed
with Intune.

114
00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:08.650
Entra ID is what Microsoft 365 and many
cloud apps use.

115
00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.700
Its old name was Azure Active Directory.

116
00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:17.950
Hybrid identity means one account works in
both, because a sync tool copies users and

117
00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.000
groups from AD DS to Entra ID.

118
00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.250
There are two sync tools.

119
00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:29.100
Microsoft Entra Connect Sync is a full
sync server you install on Windows Server,

120
00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:30.950
with its own database.

121
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.800
It can also set up pass-through
authentication and federation.

122
00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.050
You must keep it current; Learn currently
says version 2.

123
00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.500
6.

124
00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:41.950
84.

125
00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:46.200
0 or later, with application-based
authentication, by April 7, 2027.

126
00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:52.050
Microsoft Entra Cloud Sync uses light
agents, and its settings live in the Entra

127
00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:53.500
admin center.

128
00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:58.150
It handles disconnected forests, and
Microsoft calls it the recommended path

129
00:07:58.400 --> 00:07:60.000
forward for most organizations.

130
00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:05.850
In SA1 you see this in my demo video;
hands-on Azure comes in Server

131
00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:07.300
Administration 2.

132
00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.350
How does Entra ID check a password?

133
00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:11.800
Three options.

134
00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:18.450
Password hash sync, PHS, sends a hash of
each password hash to Entra ID, and Entra

135
00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.300
ID checks sign-ins itself.

136
00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:24.550
If the head office goes offline, cloud
sign-in still works.

137
00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.200
Pass-through authentication, PTA, uses
agents on your servers to check passwords

138
00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:32.650
against AD DS, so the check stays
on-premises.

139
00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:37.300
Federation hands the whole sign-in to your
own AD FS servers.

140
00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:42.350
With PTA or federation, if the head office
is offline, sign-in fails.

141
00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:48.200
That is why Microsoft advises turning on
PHS whatever you choose, as a backup.

142
00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:51.650
Ann Lee signs in as alee at sa1.

143
00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.100
lab.

144
00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:56.550
That is her user principal name, or UPN.

145
00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.800
Entra Connect only syncs users to domains
verified in the tenant, and only a real

146
00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.050
internet domain can be verified.

147
00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.500
sa1.

148
00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:13.150
lab is not one, so a synced user would
land on an onmicrosoft dot com name.

149
00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:18.600
A real organization first adds a routable
domain it owns, for example example.

150
00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:23.650
com, as an alternative UPN suffix in
Active Directory Domains and Trusts.

151
00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:29.900
Then it verifies the domain in Entra ID,
changes each user's UPN, and only then

152
00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:31.350
syncs.

153
00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:32.800
Four mistakes.

154
00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.050
One: leaving out dash ReadOnlyReplica.

155
00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:40.900
You get a writable DC instead, so read the
command before you press Enter.

156
00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:45.150
Two: allowing Domain Admins so admins can
sign in offline.

157
00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:50.600
Deny wins anyway, and you would expose
your most powerful passwords; delegate a

158
00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.450
branch admin group instead.

159
00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:57.900
Three: expecting a password on the RODC
right after you allow the group.

160
00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:03.350
Allowed means may be cached, so
prepopulate or wait for a sign-in, then

161
00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.200
check the stored list.

162
00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:08.250
Four: syncing users with an at sa1.

163
00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:09.700
lab UPN.

164
00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:12.750
Add and verify a routable suffix first.

165
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.600
Time to check yourself.

166
00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:19.250
SRV-CORE is an RODC, and GG-Students is on
its Allowed list.

167
00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:24.700
First, a branch has twelve users, an
unlocked closet and a slow link.

168
00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:27.350
Writable DC or RODC, and why?

169
00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.200
Second, a Domain Admins member signs in at
SRV-CORE.

170
00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:33.050
Is the password cached?

171
00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:38.500
Third, dpatel is in GG-Students, but the
stored list does not show him.

172
00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:41.950
Why, and how do you fix it now?

173
00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:47.400
And fourth, cloud sign-in must keep
working when the head office is offline.

174
00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:48.850
Which sign-in option?

175
00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:50.700
Pause the video now.

176
00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:52.550
Here are the answers.

177
00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.000
One: an RODC.

178
00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:59.850
With weak physical security and a slow
link, you want a DC that nothing

179
00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:04.900
replicates out of, and that stores no
passwords unless you allow them.

180
00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:06.350
Two: no.

181
00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:11.800
Domain Admins is in the Denied RODC
Password Replication Group, and deny wins,

182
00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:15.250
so DC01 checks every one of those
sign-ins.

183
00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:18.300
Three: allowed does not mean cached yet.

184
00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:22.950
Use Prepopulate Passwords, or wait for his
first sign-in through SRV-CORE.

185
00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:27.600
Four: password hash sync, because Entra ID
checks the password itself.

186
00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:33.050
On SRV-CORE, I install the AD DS role and
run Install-ADDSDomainController with dash

187
00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:34.900
ReadOnlyReplica and dash SiteName.

188
00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:39.150
I type the passwords at the prompts and
answer Y.

189
00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:43.000
After the restart, DC01 lists SRV-CORE
with IsReadOnly True.

190
00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:47.250
In ADUC, the Password Replication Policy
tab shows the defaults.

191
00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.500
I add GG-Students with Allow.

192
00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:53.350
Under Advanced, only the RODC's own
accounts are stored.

193
00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:56.800
The Resultant Policy allows dpatel but not
alee.

194
00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:01.850
I prepopulate dpatel, and PowerShell
confirms his password is now stored on

195
00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:03.300
SRV-CORE.

196
00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:09.150
Nobody else from GG-Students is cached
yet, and that is exactly what the policy

197
00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:10.600
promised.

198
00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:12.050
To wrap up.

199
00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:15.900
Two writable DCs remove the single point
of failure.

200
00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:20.950
An RODC holds every object but no
passwords by default, and nothing

201
00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:22.800
replicates out of it.

202
00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:26.250
Install-ADDSDomainController with dash
ReadOnlyReplica and dash SiteName builds

203
00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:27.700
one.

204
00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:32.350
In the PRP, deny wins, and allowed means
may be cached.

205
00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:37.800
Entra Connect Sync or Cloud Sync copies AD
DS users to Entra ID.

206
00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:43.650
Turn on password hash sync as a backup,
and use a routable UPN suffix.

207
00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:45.100
Lab 4.

208
00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:50.950
4 practices the RODC part in about sixty
minutes, plus a short hybrid identity

209
00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:52.400
scenario.
